figdown 0.1 block
# `SCENE-KEYWORD-MEMBERSHIP`/`PAINT-ORDER-CONSTRUCT`: the cells WITHDRAWN from `block`. There are two,
# and they are withdrawn by DIFFERENT rulings with different diagnostics —
# which is the whole point of writing them in one file. The KEEP half of the
# grid for this genre is 245.
#
#   bundle  `SCENE-KEYWORD-MEMBERSHIP`, a PER-GENRE withdrawal. `bundle` is still live under
#           `topology` and this line would parse there. It had ZERO authored
#           uses under `block`, and the construct is defined by a REFERENT
#           only that genre has — a LAG (IEEE 802.1AX), an ECMP set, an EVPN
#           Ethernet Segment. Under `block` it was a ring drawn round
#           parallel edges with nothing to name. The message must say so:
#           a word withdrawn from one genre is not a misspelling, and
#           `"bundle" is not allowed in genre block` on its own would send an
#           author hunting for a typo that is not there.
#
#   plane   `PAINT-ORDER-CONSTRUCT`, a LANGUAGE-WIDE withdrawal — not per genre, so it reports a
#           different message reached from a different place: `plane` joins
#           the retired-keyword sweep AHEAD of the `GENRE-KEYWORD-ALLOWLIST` genre allowlist, and
#           answers identically in every genre, including `bitfield`,
#           `table` and `timing`. It is written here so the block column of
#           the grid is complete in one file; the full sweep, and the two
#           option keys that went with it (`plane=`, `z-index=`), are pinned
#           by 918-withdrawn-plane-keyword.
#
# Both withdrawals were FREE: in `block` — the one normative scene genre —
# `bundle` and `plane` were the EXPERIMENTAL keywords of the keyword-status table,
# and `EDGE-GEOMETRY-CONSTRUCTS` (`path`/`routing`) is the precedent for withdrawing an
# experimental construct with no version gate and no rewrite owed. `group`
# and `external` are the only normative cells in this column and neither
# moved.
node a "A"
node b "B"
edge a -- b
bundle lag "LAG" a--b
plane over "Overlay"
